\begin_layout Subsubsection
Accounting for multiple comparisons
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Due to the large number of outcome variables in the present study, false
 positives are a potential concern when conventional approaches to statistical
 inference are used.
 We employ two strategies to avoid this problem, following broadly the approache
s of Kling et al.
 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
citeyear{kling_experimental_2007}
\end_layout

\end_inset

, Anderson 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
citeyear{anderson_multiple_2008}
\end_layout

\end_inset

, and Casey et al.
 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
citeyear{casey_reshaping_2012}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First, we compute standardized indices for several main groups of outcomes,
 and choose focal variables of interest for others (all specified in the
 PAP), as described above.
 Second, even after collapsing variables into indices and choosing focal
 variables of interest for each group of outcomes, we are still left with
 multiple indices, creating the need to further control the probability
 of Type I errors.
 To this end, we control the family-wise error rate (FWER; 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
shortciteNP{westfall_adjusting_1993,efron_introduction_1993,anderson_multiple_20
08,casey_reshaping_2012}
\end_layout

\end_inset

) across the treatment coefficients on the indices for our main outcome
 groups, that is assets, consumption, income, psychological wellbeing, education
, food security, health, and female empowerment.
 As specified in our PAP, we apply this correction to the index variables
 only; when discussing individual variable results within particular outcome
 groups, we use conventional significance levels.
 We use this approach because the purpose of studying individual variables
 within the outcome groups is to understand mechanisms, rather than to single
 out particular variables for general conclusions.
 Further detail on controls for multiple inference is given in Online Appendix
 Section 3.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Minimum detectable effect sizes 
\emph on
(MDEs)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For null results, we report the 
\emph on
minimum detectable effect size
\emph default
 
\emph on
(MDE) 
\emph default
with 80 percent power at a significance level of 0.05.
 It is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
MDE=(t_{1-\kappa}+t_{\alpha/2})\times\frac{\sigma}{\sqrt{NP(1-P)}},
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{1-\kappa}$
\end_inset

 is the value of the 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

-statistic required to obtain 80 percent power, 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{\alpha/2}$
\end_inset

 is the critical 
\emph on
t
\emph default
-value required to achieve a significance level of 0.05, 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 is the fraction of the sample that were treated, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\sigma}{\sqrt{NP(1-P)}}$
\end_inset

 is the standard error of the treatment coefficient.
 With 
\begin_inset Formula $P=0.5$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{1-\kappa}=0.84$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{\alpha/2}=1.96$
\end_inset

, this expression simplifies to a simple multiple of the standard error
 of the treatment coefficient, 
\begin_inset Formula $SE(\hat{\beta})$
\end_inset

: 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
MDE=2.8\times SE(\hat{\beta})
\]

\end_inset

We use this formula to compute the 
\emph on
MDE 
\emph default
for null results.
 In the case of outcome variables with a non-zero control group mean (such
 as monetary variables), we additionally report the 
\emph on
MDE 
\emph default
as a proportion of the control group mean.
 
\end_layout

