\begin_layout Standard
In the following section we assess the impact of transfers on assets and
 investment, and explore average returns on investment and the possibility
 of savings and credit constraints.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We begin by estimating the treatment effect on asset ownership in Panel
 A of Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:assets_ent_final_comprehensive_w_in"

\end_inset

, measured by asking respondents for the present value of a number of common
 assets.
 The variables reported here are a subset of those pre-specified; full results
 are reported in the Online Appendix (Tables 49–62).
 
\emph on
MDEs
\emph default
 are shown in Online Appendix Table 23.
 The overall treatment effect on assets amounts to USD 302 PPP relative
 to a control group mean of 495 (61 percent), and is mainly driven by investment
 in livestock and durables.
 Livestock holdings increase by USD 83 PPP, a 50 percent increase relative
 to the control group mean, and 12 percent of the average transfer.
 Similarly, the value of durable goods owned by treatment households increased
 by USD 53 PPP relative to a control group mean of USD 207 PPP (an increase
 of 25 percent, or 7 percent of the average transfer), primarily due to
 purchases of furniture (beds, chairs, tables, etc.).
 Reported cash savings balances doubled as a result of cash transfers, but
 from low initial levels (baseline mean of USD 11 PPP).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One of the most visible impacts of the transfer is investment in metal roofs.
 Cash transfers increase the likelihood of having a metal roof by 24 percentage
 points relative to a control group mean of 16 percent at endline.
 Because the average cost of a metal roof is USD 669 PPP, this effect correspond
s to a USD 161 PPP increase in the value of roofs owned by treatment households,
 which in turn corresponds to 23 percent of the average transfer.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Incidentally, the fact that a large number of transfer recipients chose
 to acquire a metal roof suggests that they understood that the program
 was transitory, because by acquiring a metal roof they disqualified themselves
 from it.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The variables reported above are for durable assets; to get a full picture
 of investments that may have financial returns, we additionally created
 an index of non-durable investment, consisting of the total spending on
 agricultural inputs (seed, fertilizer, water, hired labor, livestock feed,
 livestock medicine, etc.), enterprise expenses (wages, electricity, water,
 transport, inventory, and other inputs), education expenditures (school
 and college fees, books, uniforms), and savings.
 Results are reported in Online Appendix Tables 143–148.
 We find an increase in non-durable investment by USD 23 PPP relative to
 a control group mean of USD 40 PPP (59 percent); on a percentage basis,
 this increase is thus very similar in magnitude to the increase in durable
