\begin_layout Standard
We now briefly discuss differences in asset holdings and business outcomes
 across treatment arms.
 First, quite naturally, we find that large transfers increase asset holdings
 by an additional USD 279 PPP relative to small transfers.
 Second, we find little evidence that asset holdings and business outcomes
 are different when transfers are made to the female rather than the male,
 except that female recipient households are 11 percentage points less likely
 to invest in metal roofs.
 However, the 
\emph on
MDEs
\emph default
 for these comparisons were large, ranging from 19 percent of the control
 group mean for durables to 129 percent for savings.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally, the randomization in transfer timing (lump-sum vs.
 monthly) allows us to ask whether households are savings- or credit-constrained
 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
shortcite{dupas_savings_2013,ashraf_tying_2006}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 Specifically, the permanent income hypothesis 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
shortcite{friedman_theory_1957,modigliani_utility_1954}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 predicts that households invest the balance of their transfers to smooth
 consumption over time.
 However, a significant portion of the transfer was spent on consumption
 goods.
 One possible explanation of this finding is that households face lumpy
 investment opportunities and are constrained in their ability to save and
 borrow; in this case, we would expect fewer purchases of expensive assets
 such as metal roofs among monthly transfer recipients, because the savings
 constraint would prevent this group from saving their transfer to buy the
 asset, and the credit constraint would prevent it from borrowing against
 the promise of future transfer.
 Indeed, in Column (4) of Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:assets_ent_final_comprehensive_w_in"

\end_inset

 we find that endline asset holdings of monthly recipient households are
 significantly lower than those of lump-sum recipients (although note that
 this effect does not survive FWER-adjustment in Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:indices_comprehensive"

\end_inset

).
 In particular, monthly recipients are 12 percentage points less likely
 to acquire a metal roof, and instead use more of the transfer for current
 consumption, evident in a significantly higher food security index (Table
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:indices_comprehensive"

\end_inset

).
 Thus, monthly recipient households may be both credit- and savings-constrained.
 The finding that lump-sum recipient households are more likely to make
 large investments mirrors that of 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
shortciteN{barrera-osorio2008}
\end_layout

\end_inset

, who find that bundling the payments of a conditional cash transfer program
 at the time when children have to re-enroll in school increases enrollment
 rates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The fact that program participation required signing up for mobile money
 accounts, which are a low-cost savings technology (people could have chosen
 to accumulate their transfer in their 
\emph on
M-Pesa 
\emph default
account, and even add additional funds), suggests that the savings constraint
 at work is more social or behavioral than due purely to the lack of access
 to a savings technology.
 Anecdotally, we know that recipients were often asked by family members
 to share the transfer.
 In the case of monthly transfers, a single installment of which can usually
 not be used to buy large, lumpy assets, these requests may be harder to
 refuse, while lump-sum transfer recipients might have an easier time arguing
 that the entire transfer is needed to pay for the planned purchase.
 An additional factor may be that households had more time to plan what
 to do with the lump-sum transfers, since they arrived halfway through the
 treatment period on average, while monthly transfers began soon after the
 announcement for all households.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The results on cortisol levels (Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:psyvars_comprehensive"

\end_inset

) provide a tantalizing complement to this interpretation: We find that
 cortisol levels are significantly higher for households who receive monthly
 transfers than for those who receive lump-sum transfers—and in fact, significan
tly elevated even relative to cortisol levels for the control group (cf.
 Online Appendix Tables 119, 128, and 135).
 This is despite the fact that immediate pressures on the lives of these
 recipients have decreased, as evident, for example, in the significant
 increase in food security.
 It is conceivable that the increase in cortisol levels for monthly transfer
 recipients reflects the stress associated with having to decide continually
 how to spend the transfers or an inability to save, while the transfers
 of lump-sum recipients are safely invested in metal roofs.
 
\end_layout

